        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Society"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Barrier"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Attraction"
        },
        {
          "category": "Tropes",
          "tag": "Enemies to Lovers"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "19d42d40-9acf-48f1-92d2-5895fc1fe685"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 1086,
      "end_time": 1093,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Taryn's decision to leave in this scene is symbolic of her choice to abandon Jude. She cannot stand by and watch the decisions Jude is making, so she leaves. She will protect herself instead of Jude, even though Jude would do anything to protect her. It is another way Cardan has isolated Jude, and ensured she feels alone. "
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Genres",
          "tag": "Romantasy"
        },
        {
          "category": "Forms",
          "tag": "Movie"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Worldbuilding"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Society"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "0c28a0c9-d79c-4825-b029-b8db3289bd0d"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 1094,
      "end_time": 1117,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Jude's refusal to bow before Cardan's will intrigues him. He is used to people listening to his authority. Jude is determined to create her own authority, and trap Cardan with it. This is a part of the moment of ritual death in the first season, because Cardan allows Jude authority over him. Once he does that, it allows Jude to crown him King of Faerie, and prevent him from escaping his duties the way he had intended."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Genres",
          "tag": "Romantasy"
        },
        {
          "category": "Forms",
          "tag": "Movie"
        },
        {
          "category": "Fantasy",
          "tag": "Worldbuilding"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Rules"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Society"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Barrier"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Attraction"
        },
        {
          "category": "Tropes",
          "tag": "Enemies to Lovers"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "1887b6ec-c393-42a4-9706-0f371e05be3c"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 1118,
      "end_time": 1146,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Her threats to Cardan make him wary of her, especially because she doesn't seem to be afraid of him, even though she should be. She is only a mortal, and making these threats against a Prince of Faerie could be considered treason. Cardan very easily could have her arrested, but he doesn't. A part of the reason is because he enjoys their animosity, and he's also intrigued by Jude and wants to know what she'll do next. "
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Genres",
          "tag": "Romantasy"
        },
        {
          "category": "Forms",
          "tag": "Movie"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Barrier"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Attraction"
        },
        {
          "category": "Tropes",
          "tag": "Enemies to Lovers"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "3dde6096-a763-4ee8-9bae-c7bf86f105d1"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 1169,
      "end_time": 1181,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Jude believes Cardan has come for his revenge for her brash words, but she doesn't think another prince will have come to visit her after her display at the tournament. This visit from Prince Dain signals a shift in status for Jude. Though she is still just a mortal, she is offered a role as a spy. Mortals are able to lie, and they are easily overlooked. They make for good assassins and spies. It is the only kind of power anyone would offer Jude, and she will gladly take it, and claw her way up the ranks. Especially as it will allow her some protection from Cardan. She reports to Prince Dain, now, and Cardan can have no impact on her. "
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Genres",
          "tag": "Romantasy"
        },
        {
          "category": "Forms",
          "tag": "Movie"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Rules"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Society"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Worldbuilding"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Barrier"
        },
        {
          "category": "Tropes",
          "tag": "Enemies to Lovers"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "333cac44-d59e-44aa-b557-7139a903722b"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 1182,
      "end_time": 1210,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Jude's decision to cover for Cardan and not confess his threats against her also indicates her attraction to him. Neither of them is willing to relinquish their animosity towards the other. Their rivalry is theirs, and theirs alone."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Genres",
          "tag": "Romantasy"
        },
        {
          "category": "Forms",
          "tag": "Movie"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Worldbuilding"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Society"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Attraction"
        },
        {
          "category": "Tropes",
          "tag": "Enemies to Lovers"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "d6243b39-673c-4893-a572-124556c13fc0"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 1211,
      "end_time": 1243,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "This scene acts as a reminder that in this world, only humans can lie. Faeries can speak around the truth, but they cannot tell a lie. They can omit a fact, but they have to be careful in their wording. Jude can lie, and that makes her powerful."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Genres",
          "tag": "Romantasy"
        },
        {
          "category": "Forms",
          "tag": "Movie"
        },
        {
          "category": "Fantasy",
          "tag": "Rules"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Worldbuilding"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "95d6cc84-bf6a-4dba-99e7-3fd4ec32f1fd"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 1244,
      "end_time": 1274,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "Jude's desire for power could also be interpreted as a desire to be on the same level as Cardan. Especially as she consistently seeks political power. She wants to control the throne of Faerie, and find a way to manipulate the crown itself. She will betray whoever it takes to ensure she is never powerless again, and Cardan would allow Jude to manipulate him just to see what she does. "
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Genres",
          "tag": "Romantasy"
        },
        {
          "category": "Forms",
          "tag": "Movie"
        },
        {
          "category": "Fantasy",
          "tag": "Rules"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Society"
        },
        {
          "category": "Romantasy",
          "tag": "Attraction"
        },
        {
          "category": "Tropes",
          "tag": "Enemies to Lovers"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "692f7556-9a91-4e5d-a79a-ce3ff938092a"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 1275,
      "end_time": 1303,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "The rules of enchantments are very important, because they allow for the confines of the magic system. There are ways around the resistance of enchantment, but the resistance acts as a safeguard, a way to defend oneself. Jude wants something more permanent, so she can protect herself from Cardan. She doesn't want him to ever have control of her. "
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
      "tags": [
        {
          "category": "Genres",
          "tag": "Romantasy"
        },
        {
          "category": "Fantasy",
          "tag": "Worldbuilding"
        },
        {
          "category": "Fantasy",
          "tag": "Rules"
        },
        {
          "category": "Fantasy",
          "tag": "Magic System"
        }
      ],
      "uuid": "cc2fd436-344c-4802-9a18-2297f1b7eab5"
    },
    {
      "start_time": 1317,
      "end_time": 1342,
      "annotation": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "The dismissive way Prince Dain speaks of Cardan foreshadows the way Cardan was neglected as a child. Later in season one, Jude will infiltrate Cardan's home and learn what his home life is like. Realising the kind of harsh punishments he faces from his older brother Balekin, helps her to understand why Cardan acts the way he does. If he isn't cruel, he is punished for his kindness. It helps Jude begin to overcome the barriers between them, because she realises that he was raised to be cruel, and he fights it when he can. He is simply not always in a position where resistance is possible. In a way, he envies Jude for her freedom. As she is mortal, no one has any expectations for what she will do. Cardan cannot escape the expectations placed on him, and when his family remembers to care about him, they have expectations of the kind of person he will be."
            }
          ]
        }
      ],
